<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Song Jiaoren</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a>–<a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Jiaoren was a Chinese revolutionary and a founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>, which he led to victory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s first national parliamentary elections in 1912–1913. He championed a cabinet system that would limit presidential power. He was shot at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> railway station in March 1913, an assassination widely blamed on President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a>, which led to the failed '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-revolution">Second Revolution</a>'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/song-jiaoren.jpg" alt="Song Jiaoren" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sung_Chiao-jen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Song Jiaoren was born and educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>. At six he began at a private school, and at seventeen he enrolled at Taoyuan Zhangjiang College. Influenced by his teachers, he made no effort to pursue the civil service examinations. In August 1902 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang">Wuchang</a> to attend the Bishop Boone Memorial School, where he met the revolutionary Huang Xing, who became a lifelong friend. From 1903 he taught at the Wuchang Normal School and became involved with local revolutionary groups.</p><p>Because of his revolutionary activities <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1904, where he studied Western political thought. In 1905, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>, he helped to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongmenghui">Tongmenghui</a>, which worked to overthrow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> and form a republic. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> to organise the Second Guangzhou Uprising.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was established at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> on 1 January 1912, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> was appointed to reform the legal system and drafted a provisional constitution, the Republic of China Interim Government Organization Act. He helped turn the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongmenghui">Tongmenghui</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>. Sun Yat-sen charged him, at the age of 30, with organising the party for the first election campaign in China. Only about 10 percent of adult males, some 40 million, could vote. The Kuomintang won 269 of 596 seats in the House of Representatives and 123 of 274 in the Senate, and Song was widely seen as a leading candidate for prime minister.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> wanted the elected assemblies protected from the influence of the presidency, and he spoke openly against the ambitions of the provisional president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a>, who by mid-1912 dominated the cabinet he had named and showed signs of seeking great executive power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 20 March 1913, travelling with colleagues to the Parliament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> was shot twice at close range at the Shanghai Railway Station by a lone gunman, Wu Shiying, hired by Ying Guixin, a Shanghai underworld figure close to the Yuan regime. He died two days later in hospital. The evidence led to Zhao Bingjun, the cabinet secretary and provisional premier. Contemporary Chinese media thought Yuan the most likely instigator, but the main conspirators were themselves killed or vanished: Ying was killed by two swordsmen on a train, and Zhao was poisoned in 1914. Yuan was never officially implicated.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>'s death the political climate degenerated and led to the failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-revolution">Second Revolution</a>. Yuan expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> from the elected assemblies in 1913, dissolved parliament in 1914, declared himself emperor in 1915 and died in 1916. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s first democratic system had not produced a stable government, and the country went through more than a decade of warlordism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Song Jiaoren” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the elections of 1912–13 Sun’s new Kuomintang, the Nationalist Party, won the largest share of seats, but its parliamentary leader <b>Song Jiaoren</b> was assassinated in March 1913, and Yuan crushed the ‘Second Revolution’ that followed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Taoyuan County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=28.9,111.48&amp;year=1902" aria-label="Taoyuan County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7461 1106 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Song Jiaoren"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7911 1376h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7911 1376h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=28.9,111.48&amp;year=1902">What was here in 1902? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
